The Mercedes-Benz C-Class C63 V8 refers to the W205 generation Mercedes-AMG C63 and C63 S, produced from 2015 to 2021 (Source [1, 7, 32]). This evaluation is based on the W205 C63/C63 S V8 relative to the current automotive market.

*   **price\_usd**: As a used performance car from 2015-2021, the price is significantly lower than a new car today, but still substantial. Used prices average around $47,576 for the C63 S (Source [32]), with a range from $29,250 to $59,500 (Source [32]). Original prices were around $64,825 - $85,895 (Source [2]). These prices are above the $35,000 market average for a new car. Score: -0.20.
*   **total\_cost\_estimate\_5yr\_usd**: High purchase price (as a used performance car), high maintenance costs, and potential for significant repair expenses contribute to a high 5-year cost of ownership (Source [10, 16, 36]). Score: -0.90.
*   **insurance\_usd\_yr**: High-performance AMG models have very high insurance costs due to power, value, and repair expense. Score: -0.90.
*   **maint\_usd\_yr**: Mercedes-AMG maintenance is generally very high due to specialized components and labor (Source [10, 16, 36]). Score: -0.90.
*   **repair\_risk\_score**: The W205 generation is generally considered reasonably reliable for a Mercedes of its era, but the M177 4.0L twin-turbo V8, while robust, can have issues like oil consumption, timing chain tensioners, and potential oil leaks (Source [9, 17]). Common W205 issues like infotainment glitches, steering rack problems, suspension wear, and transmission rough shifting have also been reported (Source [2, 9, 17]). The electronic rear differential on the S model is expensive if it fails (Source [10]). Risk is higher than a top-tier reliable brand and higher than standard C-Class models. Score: -0.50.
*   **hp**: 469 hp (C63) or 503 hp (C63 S) (Source [1, 2, 3, 4, 8, 14, 20, 25]). This is significantly above the average car today. Score: 0.90.
*   **torque**: 650 Nm (479 lb-ft) (C63) or 700 Nm (516 lb-ft) (C63 S) (Source [1, 2, 3, 4, 8, 14, 20, 25]). Also well above average. Score: 0.90.
*   **cylinder\_count**: V8 (8 cylinders) (Source [1, 2, 3, 4, 8, 14, 20]). Above the common 4 or 6 cylinders. Score: 0.50.
*   **engine\_displacement**: 4.0L (3982 cc) V8 (Source [1, 2, 3, 4, 8, 14, 20]). Large displacement by modern standards. Score: 0.50.
*   **accel\_0\_60\_sec**: Around 3.9-4.1 seconds (0-62 mph) (Source [4]). Carwow measured 3.0 seconds for the C63 S (Source [27]). Very fast acceleration, comparable to many modern performance cars. Score: 0.90.
*   **top\_speed**: Electronically limited to 155 mph (250 km/h), with an optional increase to 180 mph (290 km/h) for the S variant (Source [4]). Score: 0.60.
*   **drive\_type**: RWD (Source [1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8, 14, 18, 20, 23, 32]). Score: 1.00 (based on guide).
*   **transmission\_type**: 7-speed AMG Speedshift MCT automatic (pre-facelift) or 9-speed AMG Speedshift MCT automatic (facelift) (Source [1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 14, 18, 20, 26, 32]). These are multi-clutch automatics. The guide lists "Automatic Transmission" as +1.00. Sticking to the guide. Score: 1.00.
*   **offroad\_ability**: Designed purely for performance, low ground clearance, stiff suspension. Score: 1.00 (based on guide examples).
*   **braking\_dist\_ft**: High-performance brakes standard, optional carbon ceramics (Source [2, 4, 14]). Car and Driver measured 154 ft from 70 mph for the C300 (Source [4]), C63 would be better. Score: 0.50.
*   **size**: Length around 4686-4758 mm (184.5-187.3 inches) (Source [4]). Around the 4600mm average. Score: 0.00.
*   **wheelbase**: 2840 mm (111.8 inches) (Source [4]). This is longer than the 2700mm average. Score: 0.20.
*   **body\_style**: Available as Sedan, Coupe, Convertible, and Estate (Source [4, 32]). Score: 0.00.
*   **seats**: 4 seats (Coupe/Convertible) or 5 seats (Sedan/Estate) (Source [20]). Score: 0.00.
*   **cargo\_space**: Sedan boot space around 435 liters (15.36 cu ft) (Source [4, 20]). Around the 15 cu ft average. Score: 0.00.
*   **rear\_space**: Improved over the previous W204 generation (Source [12]), but still not outstanding. Score: 0.00.
*   **ground\_clearance\_in**: Around 92mm (3.62 inches) (Source [4]). Low for a sedan by modern standards. Score: -0.20.
*   **turn\_radius\_ft**: Around 11.22m (36.81 ft) (Source [4]). Around the 36 ft average. Score: 0.00.
*   **mpg**: Combined EPA around 17-20 MPG (Source [4, 21]). Real-world MPG is often lower (around 14.5-27.5 mpg reported) (Source [5, 19, 21]). This is below the 30 mpg average. Score: -0.80.
*   **co2\_g\_km**: Around 200-218 g/km (Source [4]). This is above the 175 g/km average. Score: -0.40.
*   **electric\_range\_mi**: Not electric. Score: -1.00.
*   **charge\_speed\_kw**: Not applicable. Score: -1.00.
*   **style\_sleek\_vs\_boxy**: W205 design is a sleek, modern sedan shape, inspired by the S-Class (Source [2, 4]). AMG styling adds aggression. Score: 0.40.
*   **style\_aggression\_rating**: Aggressive AMG styling with unique grille, bumpers, and wider fenders on the coupe (Source [2, 4]). Score: 0.80.
*   **style\_modernity\_rating**: Design is from the mid-2010s. Looks somewhat dated compared to the current W206, but still reasonably modern. Score: -0.20.
*   **handling\_rating**: Praised for sharp handling, agility, and high grip levels, especially the S model with the electronic LSD (Source [2, 3, 6, 12, 14, 25]). Score: 0.80.
*   **ride\_comfort\_rating**: Ride quality is firm, especially on larger wheels and in sportier modes, but generally considered acceptable for a performance car, and better than the previous W204 C63 (Source [2, 3, 6, 12, 15, 16]). Score: 0.40.
*   **cabin\_noise\_rating**: The V8 engine and exhaust are prominent (Source [1, 3, 5, 12]), but cabin insulation is decent for a performance car. Score: -0.20.
*   **shift\_smooth\_rating**: The MCT transmission is generally quick and responsive, especially the later 9-speed (Source [1, 2, 16, 17, 26]). Can be a bit clunky at low speeds or when cold (Source [16, 17]). Score: 0.00.
*   **steering\_feel\_rating**: Steering is precise and well-weighted, but some reviews note a lack of feel compared to rivals like the BMW M3 (Source [2, 6, 11, 16, 24]). Score: -0.50.
*   **adas\_score**: Offered a good range of ADAS for its time, including Collision Prevention Assist, Blind Spot Assist, and optional Distronic Plus (Source [2, 14, 23]). Score: 0.20.
*   **visibility\_rating**: Standard sedan/coupe visibility. Score: 0.00.
*   **headlight\_rating**: LED headlights were standard or available (Source [2]). Good for their time, but not modern adaptive matrix LEDs. Score: 0.20.
*   **security\_rating**: Standard for a luxury performance car of its time, with alarm and immobiliser (Source [20]). Optional enhanced security features might have been available. Score: 0.00.
*   **screen\_in**: Featured a tablet-style screen, typically around 7-8.4 inches with COMAND Online (Source [2, 25]). Facelift models gained a larger standard screen (Source [2]). Average for its time, but small by modern standards. Score: -0.20.
*   **infotainment\_ux\_rating**: COMAND Online was advanced for its time, but the interface is less intuitive than modern systems (Source [2, 9]). Apple CarPlay/Android Auto were added later (Source [2]). Score: -0.20.
*   **smartphone\_connect\_rating**: Bluetooth, USB standard. Apple CarPlay/Android Auto added later in the W205's life (Source [2, 29, 31]). Score: 0.00.
*   **audio\_rating**: Optional Burmester system was excellent (Source [2, 23]). Standard system is likely average. Score: 0.40.
*   **usb\_ports**: Likely one or two ports (Source [29]). Score: -0.50.
*   **comfort\_feature\_score**: Heated front seats standard, power seats with memory, dual-zone climate control, ambient lighting available (Source [2, 14, 18, 20, 23]). Optional ventilated and AMG Performance seats available (Source [2, 14, 18, 23]). Good level of comfort features for its era. Score: 0.60.
*   **convenience\_feature\_score**: Keyless entry/start, rearview camera standard, parking sensors available (Source [2, 14, 20, 23]). Lacks some modern convenience tech like 360 cameras as standard on early models. Score: 0.20.
*   **brand\_prestige\_rating**: Mercedes-AMG. High prestige. Score: 0.90.
*   **model\_prestige\_rating**: C Class is mid-range, but C63 is a high-performance variant (Source [1, 14, 24]). Score: -0.50.
*   **trim\_prestige\_rating**: C63 and C63 S are top-tier performance trims for the W205 C-Class (Source [1, 14, 24, 32]). Score: -1.00.
*   **interior\_luxury\_rating**: W205 interior was a significant improvement in quality and design over the W204, feeling very premium for its time (Source [2, 14]). AMG models added sport seats, carbon fiber, and unique trim (Source [2, 14, 23]). Materials and design are somewhat dated compared to the latest luxury interiors. Score: 0.60.
*   **interior\_tech\_focus\_rating**: Featured a prominent screen and COMAND Online, with a digital instrument cluster added later (Source [2, 25]). Represented a good tech focus for its era, but less screen-dominated than the current W206. Score: 0.40.

